    id: "eng-2010-hl-q4-a", topicId: "eng-1-3", conceptId: "any-following",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2010 HL Q4(a)",
    questionText: "Explain any two of the following:\n(i) Dislocation defect;\n(ii) Age hardening;\n(iii) Intermetallic compound;\n(iv) Eutectoid point.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 16,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "This line defect results from an incomplete layer of atoms in a crystal structure. Dislocations can cause a weakening of the structure as the application of a stress will move the dislocation and result in early failure.", marks: 16, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":8,"options":["This line defect results from an incomplete layer of atoms in a crystal structure. Dislocations can cause a weakening of the structure as the application of a stress will move the dislocation and result in early failure.","Aluminium alloyed with copper and cooled from a high temperature will increase in hardness over time at room temperature. This is due to the precipitation of CuAl2. Age hardening is a feature of a range of alloys, especially non-ferrous combinations.","Intermetallic compounds exist as solid phases containing two or more metallic elements and may have other non-metallic elements. Intermetallic compounds will exhibit a different crystal structure than its elements. They are generally brittle with a high melting point. An example is iron carbide or cementite.","A solid to solid change point on the iron-carbon diagram that allows solid pearlite to change to austenite at 0.83% carbon and 723ºC."]} },
    ],
