    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Engineering 2014 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "eng-2014-hl-q1-h", topicId: "eng-3-14", conceptId: "discuss-contribution-any-following-has-made",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2014 HL Q1(h)",
    stem: "Section A – 50 marks Give brief answers to any ten of the following:",
    questionText: "Discuss the contribution that any one of the following has made to technology: (i) Jack Kilby (ii) Marie Curie (iii) John Dunlop.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed"}, totalMarks: 5,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "Jack Kilby Jack Kilby (1923 – 2005) was an American electrical engineer who took part in the realization of the first integrated circuit while working at Texas Instruments in 1958. He was awarded the Nobel Prize in physics in 2000. He is also the inventor of the handheld calculator and the thermal printer.", marks: 5, contextNote: "The scheme's note on Marie Curie is shown as printed.", group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":5,"options":["Jack Kilby Jack Kilby (1923 – 2005) was an American electrical engineer who took part in the realization of the first integrated circuit while working at Texas Instruments in 1958. He was awarded the Nobel Prize in physics in 2000. He is also the inventor of the handheld calculator and the thermal printer.","Marie Curie (1867 – 1934) was a Polish and naturalized-French physicist and chemist who conducted pioneering research on radioactivity and discovered two elements - polonium and radium. She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize and the only person to win in multiple sciences.","John Dunlop. John Dunlop (1840 – 1921) was a Scottish inventor who moved to Northern Ireland. He was one of the founders of a rubber company and is recognised for the development of the pneumatic tyre."]} },
    ],
